benzene (MoHex 2.0) on a modern toolchain, and the genmove crashes.

Upstream (github.com/cgao3/benzene-vanilla-cmake) has been unmaintained since
2015. These fixes are needed to build it and to survive a full 13x13 game.

A theme runs through all of them: benzene guards its invariants with
`BenzeneAssert`, which compiles out in a release build. Every crash below is a
place where the assert was the only thing standing between a "cannot happen"
state and undefined behaviour.

1. src/util/Misc.cpp -- Boost.Filesystem v2 calls (`branch_path`, `normalize`)
   that Boost removed; replaced by the v3 spellings (needs Boost >= 1.60).

2. src/hex/Resistance.cpp -- a segfault, hit several times per 13x13 game.
   ComputeScores() indexes `pointToIndex[source]`, but that entry is only
   assigned while iterating the groups *excluding* the sink's. When ICE
   fill-in inside the search connects the two edges of `color`, source and
   sink share a captain, the entry is never written, and the uninitialised
   value is used as an array index. Guard the already-connected case (the
   position is won for `color`: zero resistance) and initialise the table.

3. src/mohex/MoHexPlayer.cpp -- `genmove` answering with an illegal move.
   When the search proves the root a win after a single simulation it returns
   an empty sequence, and the fallback asks the *fill-in* board for a random
   empty cell -- but in a proven-won position the fill-in can cover every
   cell, so the fallback is INVALID_POINT and the engine plays "invalid".
   Fall back to the real position (preferring the consider set) instead.

Applied by scripts/fetch-mohex.sh.

4. src/hex/HexPoint.cpp -- ToString() indexes the name table with the point it
   was handed, guarded only by an assert. Callers include the error paths that
   *report* a bad move, so a bad point crashed the engine instead of being
   named. Reporting a bad move must never be worse than making one: bounds-check
   and return "?".

5. src/hex/EndgameUtil.cpp + src/hex/BenzenePlayer.cpp -- genmove answering with
   an occupied cell, or dying.

   MostOverlappingMove() leaves `curBestMove` at -1 when no cell in the
   intersection appears in any carrier, then returns (HexPoint)-1. That value is
   used as an array index by the code reporting it (see 4), so the engine
   segfaulted mid-error-message -- which is why it looked like a silent death.
   It now reports INVALID_POINT and guesses nothing.

   PlayWonGame() has a "should never get here" branch that likewise returns
   INVALID_POINT once its assert is gone, and it is reached constantly in
   heavily filled-in positions (65 times per 8 games at 100 playouts).

   Both leave the determined-state path with no move. Rather than substitute a
   random cell -- which in a *proven won* position can throw the win away --
   CheckEndgame() now falls through to a real UCT search, filling in the
   consider set that the determined branch never populated.

6. src/mohex/MoHexEngine.cpp -- a last-resort legality check. Whatever the
   internals decide, the move must be legal in the *game* board before it is
   returned. Note this cannot be checked deeper in the stack: those layers hold
   the ICE fill-in board, where a cell can be occupied and still be perfectly
   legal in the real position.

Verified: 8-game matches at 400 sims / 100 playouts, previously losing about
half their games to crashes, now complete 8 of 8 with no illegal moves and no
random substitutions -- confirmed on a seed not used during debugging.

